<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bulawayo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bulawayo is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, in the south-west of the country. It began in the 1870s as the royal town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, and was rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> after its occupation in 1893. It grew into a major railway and industrial centre. The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, a capital of the Torwa state, lie nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulawayo.jpg" alt="Bulawayo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prince Phumulani Nyoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bulawayo_CBD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
